hi  so today i wanted to talk about the book  by lisa feldman called how emotions are  made and i wanted to start this as the  first book in my series on a few  neuroscience books that i want to read  this year because i want to get a little  bit wider on my knowledge and  information on neuroscience books so if  you are also interested in reading more  about neuroscience philosophy or  psychology i hope you enjoy these videos  so yeah the first book i want to talk  about is this week is my lisa feldman  and it's called how emotions are made  and i thought it was a really good book  like i gave it four stars i think on  goodreads yeah so in this book i will  dive into five lessons or five ideas i  got from the book so emotions are used  everywhere we teach kids and  psychologists with these standard  emotions autistic people are trained  using these emotions tech companies say  they can read people's emotions nowadays  using complicated ai algorithms and they  are even used during court but years of  research have shown that there are no  such things as universal emotions not in  faces not in bodies and not in the core  circuits of our brain  so the first idea that lisa feldman  delves into a little bit is the lack of  a core fingerprint in of emotions in our  faces bodies and our brains so in  general when we think of emotions we  usually think of certain emotional  stereotypes in our faces so for example  when we're happy we would smile or when  we're angry we would frown but it has  actually been shown that in natural  settings so settings outside people  don't really react like this so when  sportsmen for example are really happy  they sometimes also scrunch their face  or look really angry and sometimes when  someone is really sad they can actually  look quite blank so it has been shown  that there are no such thing as actually  universal emotions in the face so the  next myth she wanted to bust was to see  if there are universal emotions in the  body so for example you can imagine when  your heart is racing and you feel your  chest cramping that could be a sign that  you're really anxious but she actually  gives the example that a lot of women  get misdiagnosed for certain heart  attacks because they are diagnosed with  having an anxiety attack and this just  shows that also the body doesn't really  have a universal signal or universal  fingerprint for certain emotions so  sometimes when someone is getting really  angry their heart rate goes up but for  other people when they get really angry  their heart rate actually drops and the  last myth she wanted to debunk is that  there's such a thing as a universal  signal in the brain and i was actually a  little bit shocked by this because we  learn for example that one of the  centers for fear in the brain is the  amygdala so the amygdala is this tiny  piece or tiny center in your brain that  is usually related to fear or a lot of  studies show that it's related to fear  but she gives this example of two twins  so it's a pair of identical twins who  lost the supposed fear-related parts of  their amygdala both were diagnosed at  the age of 12 have normal intelligence  and have a high school education despite  their identical dna equivalent brain  damage and common environment both as  children and adults twins have very  different profiles regarding fear one of  the trin actually has fear related  deficits and doesn't experience much  regarding fear whereas the other twin  basically has normal responses during  fear so it kind of shows that one of the  twins doesn't have any related responses  to fear and probably her brain networks  don't show up normally as would be for  fear whereas the other twin her brain  kind of compensates for the general fact  that her amygdala is damaged and this  shows that there is in the brain not  really one area that encompasses fear  it's actually different parts of the  brain that work together that create the  experience of feeling fear so lisa  feldman kind of goes into this idea of  the bayesian brain so the bayesian brain  is the fact that our past experience  shapes how we think of our future  experience or that our prior  probabilities are updated by new data  and our prior probabilities is kind of  the biases we have so lisa feldman then  says that our emotion experience is made  up of the things that come from inside  the things that come from outside and  our past experience with certain type of  emotions so for example if you're  feeling your heart race and you're kind  of sweaty your outside experiences that  you have a presentation and you know  from past experience that you get really  excited for presentations you probably  would conclude that your emotion at that  point is excitement and i find this idea  kind of interesting that the emotions  that you feel don't really have a ground  truth but it's more how you from past  experience decide what your emotion is  at that moment and then the third thing  she goes into is that language culture  and emotion are then highly correlated  so she describes this disorder called  alexotymia or emotional blindness and  this is a broad term to describe certain  problems with feeling emotions and it's  also the greek term for no words of  emotions and i thought it was a really  interesting type of symptomology that i  hadn't really heard of before and i  wanna describe a little article that i  read in the new york times about a girl  that experienced this so it's called my  quest for sadness so the girl says the  following i had followed her into the  bathroom and hugged her i had known aunt  lydia for 10 years she had always been  vibrant beautiful and funny but cancer  was stealing her away from us i  understood cognitively then i was sad  but as was typical for me i didn't feel  sad rather i felt matter of fact and i  think this case describes really well  what people usually with this type of  disorder feel so they can recognize  emotions from the outside and they know  that what they are feeling is not  appropriate for what's happening but  still their brain cannot conjure the  emotion of sadness in this case so lisa  feldman goes into the fact that language  and how we view emotion are then highly  correlated and highly linked so if we  don't have certain words for certain  emotions we probably won't recognize  those emotions properly so for example  the difference between jealousy and envy  a lot of us usually mix these up but  they actually describe quite different  emotional experiences dad you shouldn't  be jealous of our poo remember it's all  about the music i'm not jealous i'm  envious jealousy is when you're worried  someone will take what you have envy is  wanting what someone else has what i  feel is envy huh  wow he's right and also something that i  then find really interesting is that in  different languages you have emotions  that are not there in english so for  example in japanese you have the word  natsukashi so that's because she kind of  describes this emotion where you're  really fond of a certain past memory  that has been gone so when you look back  on a certain memory you get matsukashi  for that memory and i think that's an  experience we have in english but we  don't really have to write words for it  another one from dutch that a lot of  people always say is the word rosella  and gazelle is kind of the word cozy but  you feel it when you're with friends or  with family or in a certain situation  you feel really the coziness of being  amongst friends and that's the word and  there's not really a direct translation  to english and the last one i want to  talk about is a word from korean which i  think is such a good word that we don't  have in english and that's the word  and that is kind of this almost physical  feeling that you feel when you're really  frustrated that it's almost suffocating  and usually it's used when someone is  doing something really silly or really  stupid and you're kind of feeling like  ah  and you really feel it in your chest  hair and i think it's so interesting  when you learn certain languages that  you will have emotion concepts for  certain emotions that you cannot express  in english so the thing then that lisa  feldman says is that we are architects  of our own experience  so we are not only a passive observer of  the things we're feeling internally and  then describe them externally but we  actually have an active role in how we  interpret the signals that come from the  outside and come from the inside so if  you interpret something as stress or  something as excitement it's entirely up  to you how you view the world and your  experiences so her last advice then is  that you can actually master your own  emotions if you're a little bit more  aware of it so i will go over some of  the tips that she gives for mastering  your emotions so the first thing she  says is that you need to have a solid  foundation so a solid foundation is  usually for your body it's just a really  boring advice of eating while exercising  and having plenty of sleep the second  one is also to have human touch so for  example seeing a lot of friends spending  more time in good environments with a  lot of plants such as the outside right  and the best tip she gets i think for  having a solid foundation is adopting a  pet i really agree with this a pet is a  really good way to manage your body  budget and the second thing she goes  into is to increase your emotional  intelligence or the words you have for  your emotions so emotional granularity  is kind of how fine grained your  understanding of certain emotional  concepts may it be your own or other  people are and people that have a better  emotional granularity are usually better  at expressing their emotions and also  understanding the emotions of others so  it's quite important to get new emotion  concepts and she says you can do this by  reading books for example which i really  love because usually there's not a good  argument for reading a lot of fiction  books but getting new emotion concepts  is a really good argument for reading  fiction books you can also do it by  watching movies or in general being a  collector of experiences that are not  your own where you're not the main  character and also one tip she gives  which i thought was really funny is to  invent your own emotion words for  certain concepts so if you're feeling  something and there's not a word for it  just to make up your own words which i  think is really interesting if you're  good at that and the last advice she  goes into is mastering your emotions at  the moment so usually it is quite easy  to look back and understand your  emotions but sometimes you're feeling  really angry or really frustrated and  you just want to master your emotions in  that moment so she says to use your body  so like to go outside and exercise or to  change location is one of the best ways  to change the internal and also external  signals for a certain emotion and then  afterwards to journal about it such that  you can re-categorize exactly what you  felt and what you experienced and you  can kind of relearn some of the emotions  so yeah those were all the lessons i  read from the book and i think in  general it was one of the best books  i've read in neuroscience so far so i  really recommend if you're doing  anything with neuroscience to read this  book and also if you're doing something  white psychology i do have to say her  last chapter went a little bit into the  emotions of animals which i don't  necessarily agree with and she also  talks a little bit about consciousness  which i think is not really the place  for in a book like this yeah in general  i super highly recommend and if you want  to read a little bit more in  neuroscience i also made a little video  about that here and otherwise see you  next week with hopefully another book in  neuroscience bye

